native/android-app/REUSE.toml, which is REUSE's mechanism for exactly
that case and is generated by the same script.

What this means if you redistribute
-----------------------------------

**Replace the branding.** A fork, a distribution package, or any build
you publish must substitute its own icon. This is the same arrangement
Firefox, Chrome and Visual Studio Code have with their open-source
builds: the code is free, the brand is not.

A `SSTVAE_BRANDING` build switch to make that a one-line change, with a
freely-licensed placeholder icon, is planned but **not implemented yet**
— see docs/todo.md. Until it exists, replacing the files listed above in
place is the way to do it; nothing reads them by any other path.

Binaries published from this repository by the project itself do include
the licensed artwork, which the license permits.

Bundled third-party components
------------------------------

The published packages contain more than this repository's code. None of
it is covered by LICENSE either, and all of it is free software. Listed
here because a package's NOTICE is where a recipient looks, and because
two of these carry obligations that only bite on redistribution.

Copied into the packages, built from pinned upstream sources by
native/cmake/:

    Qt 6                LGPL-3.0-only          https://www.qt.io/
    Hamlib              LGPL-2.1-or-later      https://hamlib.github.io/
    libheif             LGPL-3.0-or-later      https://github.com/strukturag/libheif
    libde265            LGPL-3.0-or-later      https://github.com/strukturag/libde265
    onnxruntime         MIT                    https://onnxruntime.ai/

**Every one of the copyleft libraries above is a separate shared library,
deliberately.** Nothing in this application statically links Qt, Hamlib,
libheif or libde265, so an operator can replace any of them with their
own build without relinking anything of ours -- which is what the LGPL
asks for and the cheapest way to give it. libheif and libde265 are
reached only through the image format plugin below, which Qt loads at run
time; the application contains no reference to them at all.

The exact upstream version and a sha256 for each is in
native/cmake/onnxruntime.cmake, native/cmake/hamlib.cmake and
native/cmake/libheif.cmake, which is where to look to obtain the
corresponding source for any published build.

Compiled into the application from sources vendored in this repository:

    native/third_party/qt-heif-plugin   LGPL-2.0-or-later  (a Qt plugin, built
                                        as a separate loadable module; see its
                                        README for provenance and for the one
                                        modification made to it)
    native/third_party/stb              MIT or public domain (dual)
    native/third_party/dr_libs          MIT-0 or public domain (dual)
    native/third_party/easyexif         BSD-2-Clause
    native/third_party/pocketfft        BSD-3-Clause
    native/third_party/nlohmann         MIT
    native/third_party/msvc-pthread     Artistic-2.0 (ours; two type
                                        definitions, written here)

Each vendored directory carries its upstream LICENSE and a README saying
which version it came from and why it is vendored rather than fetched.

**HEVC patents are not a licensing matter and are not addressed by any of
the above.** The HEIF/HEIC support decodes HEVC, which several patent
pools assert claims over; that is why web browsers do not ship an HEVC
decoder and why some distributions keep libde265 out of their main
archives. Anyone publishing builds from this repository is making that
call for themselves. `-DSSTVAE_BUILD_HEIF=OFF` declines it, costs nothing
else, and the application then simply reports no HEIC support.

